They have sort of like a
Latin flare to the music.

00:35:47.566 --> 00:35:49.150
♪ Ole, ole ♪

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:51.760
Yeah, I mean-
Toyota.

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:56.520
You know, like Pitbull is kinda like a...

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:58.550
Pitbull and everything
surrounding Pitbull.

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:01.080
Chris and Ashley, they
can pump some Pitbull.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:04.210
Like I can't see you and
Jessie pumping some Pitbull.

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:06.110
The funny thing is-
Volitionally.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.973
I don't know what it
comes from, but I have a,

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:16.170
I don't like to impose
myself in places, right?

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:18.310
I don't know what it is, a lot
of tall guys have this thing

00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:19.430
where like we're a little bit hunched

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:20.900
and we're trying to make
ourselves a little bit smaller.

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:22.690
We don't like to draw
attention to ourselves.

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:25.730
So the idea of having a speaker

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:27.960
that's bringing the party wherever we go.

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:30.250
Now, first of all, very common down there,

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:31.790
but that took a little
bit of an adjustment,

00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:34.350
but eventually I just realized
that we would go by people

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:35.440
and everybody would be like,

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:36.680
they immediately began dancing to it.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:37.840
Start dancing?

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:39.821
Oh, you were like fireball.

00:36:39.821 --> 00:36:41.290
And I was like, "We are
doing the right thing.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:42.470
They've got this figured out."

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:43.830
Yeah, they've done the reconnaissance.

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:46.133
But we did not have a pirate ship.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.940
There was a pirate ship
on the second night.

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.010
I didn't expect you to.

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:53.160
We're walking up and down,

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:56.760
and then all of a sudden hear
the loudest music possible

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:57.740
getting closer to us.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:02.660
And then I look and there is
a float that is a pirate ship.

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:03.670
Like a parade float?

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:06.220
Like a parade float, but a pirate ship

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:08.530
that goes all the way down to
the ground like a low rider.

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:09.363
You couldn't even see the wheels.

00:37:09.363 --> 00:37:12.000
It would look like a pirate
ship just sliding down the road.

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:14.730
And then it has the masts of the ship

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:16.160
are basically stripper poles.

00:37:16.160 --> 00:37:17.710
Let's just be honest.
Perfect.

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:21.840
And I'm showing you a
video of that right now.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:23.230
You'll have to watch
the video version, Link,

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:24.610
for you to see this 'cause I filmed it.

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:25.770
We actually followed it.

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:28.770
And there's just this old dude.

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:30.450
He's the only one up there dancing.

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:31.910
And then there's several old couples

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:34.150
who were back in the
back kind of driving it.

00:37:34.150 --> 00:37:35.270
And the funny thing is,

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:36.727
is they went past and Chris was like,

00:37:36.727 --> 00:37:38.270
"That might be the chamber of commerce."

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:41.270
Like he wasn't joking.
You know what I'm saying?

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:42.850
Like that's what we're dealing with.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:45.840
Could you get on board and do a dance?

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:46.673
We thought about it.
Was that

00:37:46.673 --> 00:37:47.506
what was gonna happen
later in the evening?

00:37:47.506 --> 00:37:50.640
Well, every stoplight more
women got on, eventually.

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.008
Oh.

00:37:52.008 --> 00:37:53.070
And I mean-
Wow.

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:54.560
Let me see.
Fantasy land.

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:55.760
I'm talking, Fantasy Fest.

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:59.730
I'm talking about like a 65-year-old man

00:37:59.730 --> 00:38:02.350
with a ship's wheel is how
he's controlling this thing.

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:03.840
Damn, he was really
steering with that shit?

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:05.110
Yes.
Oh God.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:06.990
And there's all these people in the back

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:08.680
who were in the age range

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:12.143
and they are blaring "WAP"

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:15.970
as loud as you possibly can imagine.

00:38:15.970 --> 00:38:19.080
And there's just like old guy
who's just like dancing to it.

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:20.710
Everybody is stopping and filming it

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:21.760
because it's so awesome.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:23.796
Yeah. "We are pirates!"

00:38:23.796 --> 00:38:26.800
That's what it stands for.

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:28.540
Okay, so we got through the bike thing,

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:32.650
but then we experienced
the test of every marriage

00:38:32.650 --> 00:38:35.070
and that is double kayaking.

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:37.900
Oh God, yes it is. That's it, isn't it?

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.330
It is.
Like I remember Kevin

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:42.120
talking about his honeymoon to New Zealand

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:46.930
and they almost died on a double kayak

00:38:46.930 --> 00:38:49.200
because they were about
to kill each other.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:52.170
If you wanna know if your
marriage is gonna last,

00:38:52.170 --> 00:38:55.801
rent a double kayak
and go on a kayak tour.

00:38:55.801 --> 00:38:57.300
And the way you know it doesn't last

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:58.450
is you kill each other.

00:38:58.450 --> 00:39:01.023
We've done this before,
we did it in Hawaii,

00:39:02.272 --> 00:39:04.750
and then now we did it in Key West.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:06.840
And we were gonna go
through the mangroves.

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:10.610
Now, first of all, there's
this beautiful mangrove.

00:39:10.610 --> 00:39:12.760
These are trees that grow in the ocean?

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:15.610
They're the ones that are
like, they're on these islands,

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:17.500
but they're kind of in the ocean as well

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:19.730
and they shoot these roots
down just into the water

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:21.210
and pull up the water.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:23.570
And they're like a environmental barrier

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:24.950
for all kinds of stuff.

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:28.400
And it's also like a nesting
area for all these sharks

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:30.700
and nurse sharks and crabs and all this.

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:32.070
So we went on like an eco tour.

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:34.610
Also in the Everglades, I think.

00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:35.443
I think so.

00:39:35.443 --> 00:39:37.010
I mean, mangroves are all over the world

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:38.550
in coastal locations, but.

00:39:38.550 --> 00:39:39.610
That sounds cool though.

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:42.370
There was tunnels like
these mangrove tunnels

00:39:42.370 --> 00:39:44.900
and we would go through the tunnels

00:39:44.900 --> 00:39:46.810
because you know, like these channels,

00:39:46.810 --> 00:39:49.800
these tide channels that
you could go through.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:53.350
And I was just thinking,
"Man, I remember in Hawaii

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:55.900
where there were some tense moments."

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:58.967
And I was like, I go in thinking,

00:39:58.967 --> 00:40:00.040
"I want my own kayak

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:01.840
and I want Jessie to have her own kayak."

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.030
And then we'd get in line

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:04.487
with the other people who are there,

00:40:04.487 --> 00:40:09.290
and the woman is like,
"If you can, please,

00:40:09.290 --> 00:40:12.180
if you have a partner, please
get into a double kayak.

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:13.060
That is what we prefer."

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:15.616
And I'm like, "Damn it, okay."

00:40:15.616 --> 00:40:18.110
And we get into the-
It increases the chances

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:21.060
of at least one person
being able to get you back

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:23.140
from wherever-
I get in the kayak

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:26.530
and I say, "I'm gonna be
in the back," you know?

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:29.180
And I'm like, "Just as a reminder."

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:31.020
And the moment that I
began talking like this,

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:33.450
it feels very mansplainy to my wife.

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:34.480
But it's not of course.

00:40:34.480 --> 00:40:37.320
But, well, I mean, I've been on kayak.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.600
I've been on kayaks a lot, right?

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:44.160
And so I'm just like, "So I'm
gonna be the, I'm the rudder,

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.310
I'll be the one who turns us.

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:49.860
You just be the motor and
I'm gonna take your lead.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:51.900
You take the lead in terms of the pace,

00:40:51.900 --> 00:40:54.130
because I can see you, you can't see me.

00:40:54.130 --> 00:40:56.500
And I just want you to naturally
just go right and left.

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:57.970
And when you need to
paddle, I'll let you know.

00:40:57.970 --> 00:41:00.020
And when you can stop, you can just stop.

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:00.853
And for a lot of this,

00:41:00.853 --> 00:41:03.025
you probably won't even need to paddle."

00:41:03.025 --> 00:41:04.860
And I can immediately, even
though I can't see her face,

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:07.130
I know that I've already started
this off on the wrong foot,

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:09.909
but I'm just trying to
establish some kind of system

00:41:09.909 --> 00:41:11.440
for us to enjoy.

00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:12.273
And you sweetened the deal

00:41:12.273 --> 00:41:14.217
by seeming like you're doing a favor.

00:41:14.217 --> 00:41:15.450
"I can do this all on my own."

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.610
And what I learned in
a short period of time

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:22.630
is that the double kayak
experience with your spouse

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:25.040
is a great analogy for
marriage in general.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:28.633
Because first of all,
you gotta stay in sync.

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:32.167
You got to know your roles

00:41:32.167 --> 00:41:35.020
and I'm not talking about like,
you know, traditional roles.

00:41:35.020 --> 00:41:37.920
I'm just saying that like,
you're dividing responsibilities

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:40.310
in some way that you've
mutually agreed upon.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:43.160
There is communication that is important.

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:46.280
Yeah, and you give your wife a stick

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:47.800
so that she can bludgeon you with it.

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:52.260
Right, now I have to say that
I believe that my marriage

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:56.120
is significantly healthier than
our double kayak experience.

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:57.803
There was no, she didn't hit me,

00:41:59.150 --> 00:42:01.240
I don't even believe that
we cursed at each other,

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:02.897
